For the data science team to refine pricing models, the schema must capture the "Willingness
To Pay" (WTP) and market competitiveness.
● Offer Logs: Capture events where a rider was shown a price but did not book
(conversion funnel).
● Competitor Benchmarking: Tables storing scraped or third-party data on competitor
fares and ETAs in the same geographic zones.
● Elasticity Metadata: Fields tracking the user's device type (iPhone users may exhibit
different price sensitivity), battery level, and historical acceptance of surge fares.
Abstract Mapping Layer: Switching Between Google
and Ola Maps
The application implements an abstract interface for mapping services to allow runtime
switching between Google Maps and Ola Maps. Ola Maps is frequently preferred for the Indian
market due to its "Crafted for India" focus, which includes more accurate landmarks, regional
nuances, and a significantly lower pricing model (up to 70% cheaper than Google in some
categories).
Implementation using the Strategy Pattern
In the Bun/TypeScript environment, the application utilizes the Strategy Design Pattern to
encapsulate mapping logic. This decouples the business logic (e.g., "Request a Route") from
the specific API implementation (e.g., "Call Google Directions API").
// Abstract Strategy for Mapping
interface IMapProvider {
getRoute(origin: LatLng, destination: LatLng): Promise<RouteResult>;
getETA(origin: LatLng, destination: LatLng): Promise<number>;
getDistance(origin: LatLng, destination: LatLng): Promise<number>;
}
// Concrete Google Maps Implementation
class GoogleMapsProvider implements IMapProvider {
private client = new Client({}); // From
@googlemaps/google-maps-services-js
async getRoute(origin, destination) { /*... */ }
}
// Concrete Ola Maps Implementation
class OlaMapsProvider implements IMapProvider {
// Uses Ola Maps REST APIs with Bearer token authentication
async getRoute(origin, destination) { /*... */ }
}
By using a MapStrategyManager, the platform can dynamically select the provider based on
several factors:
● Cost Efficiency: Using Ola Maps as the default for high-volume routing requests in Tier-1
cities to minimize API fees.
